<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<Pathway xmlns="http://pathvisio.org/GPML/2021" title="UDP-α-D-galacturonate biosynthesis II (from D-galacturonate)" organism="cellular organisms" source="PlantCyc" version="20260304101735">
  <Xref identifier="PWY-4" dataSource="MetaCyc" />
  <Description>&lt;i&gt;Supporting evidence for this pathway in Oryza sativa Japonica Group&lt;/i&gt;: Enzyme(s) that catalyzes one or more of the key reactions in this pathway were predicted in Oryza sativa Japonica Group. &lt;A href=&quot;http://www.plantcyc.org/about/savi-pipeline&quot;&gt;[more info]&lt;/A&gt; &lt;i&gt;Summary from MetaCyc:&lt;/i&gt; &lt;b&gt;General Background&lt;/b&gt; Galacturonate (GalA) is a major sugar residue of plant pectic polysaccharides |CITS:[MOHNEN02]| and a minor component of some plant arabinogalactan proteins (Darvill et al., 1980; Yates et al., 1996). GalA is also found in various cell-surface polysaccharides of different Gram negative bacteria (for review see |CITS:[15563616]|). The existence of GalA is not restricted to Gram-negative bacteria being present in the capsular polysaccharide of the Gram-positive human pathogen type-I |FRAME:TAX-1313|, as well as in certain polysaccharides of the &lt;i&gt;Cyanobacterium&lt;/i&gt; spp.. The synthesis of GalA-containing polymers requires UDP-&amp;alpha;-D-galacturonate (UDP-GalA). The formation of UDP-D-GalA occurs via two routes: a) a salvage pathway leading to the formation of UDP-D-GalA from free galacturonate (this pathway), and b) a &lt;i&gt;de novo&lt;/i&gt; synthesis route involving the epimerisation of UDP-D-glucuronate by a UDP-D-glucuronate 4-epimerase (|FRAME:PWY-4861|). In plants, the primary role of the sugar salvage pathways is presumed to be in the recycling of sugars derived from the turnover of macromolecules such as polysaccharides, galactolipids and proteoglycans, which have sugar components. &lt;b&gt;Key Notes on the Enzymes:&lt;/b&gt; The galacturonokinase and UDP-D-galacturonate pyrophosphorylase activities characterizing this pathway have been found in plants from extracts of |FRAME:TAX-3916|; unfortunately, further enzymatic characterization is not available at this time.</Description>
